$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { Hints: } n \times \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3=\frac{4}{3} \pi \mathrm{R}^3 \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{R}=r n^{1 / 3} \\
& \mathrm{C}_0=4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r \\
& q_0=\mathrm{C}_0 \mathrm{~V}=\left(4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r\right) \mathrm{V}
\end{aligned}
$$
Capacitance of Bigger drop,
$$
\mathrm{C}=4 \pi \varepsilon_0 \mathrm{R}
$$
So, $\mathrm{V}=\frac{n q_0}{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{n\left(4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r \mathrm{~V}\right)}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0 \mathrm{R}}=n\left(\frac{r}{\mathrm{R}}\right) \mathrm{V}=n\left(\frac{1}{n^{1 / 3}}\right) \mathrm{V}=n^{2 / 3} \mathrm{~V}$
